Notebook[{
Cell["\<\
Controlled responses and finite-support observability limits in Rule 30\
\>", "Title",
 CellID->874461601,ExpressionUUID->"22a1a762-10e3-861e-525e-3f4ca097ed64"],

Cell["Troy Lowry; OpenAI Codex \[Bullet] 2026-10-24", "Author",
 CellID->36768919,ExpressionUUID->"4da796ef-9f57-a4bf-7b5c-0fc91ae30e85"],

Cell["\<\
Responses of a right half to every left-boundary input are characterized \
exactly by two observations of a triangular diagonal map. Terminal rewrites \
011 to 01 and 101 to 11 preserve the complete center trace with every common \
initial left half. Explicit eight-periodic infinite tails show that OR alone \
need not recover the head and that a period-two head need not detect a full \
period-eight state. Finite feedback can erase an initial distinction even \
when the common output is not eventually constant. Finite-tail completeness \
and the Rule 30 Prize problems remain open.\
\>", "Abstract",
 CellID->1699946459,ExpressionUUID->"1b39f900-6440-cee7-4ac6-f16a0dfa94cd"],

Cell["Scope and provenance", "Section",
 CellID->1535504786,ExpressionUUID->"75d93363-5da6-51a4-23e6-ab4d5453238e"],

Cell["\<\
This is a partial contribution developed by Troy Lowry with OpenAI Codex. It \
builds on the kit's driven right half in Rule30Ether and whole-line evolution \
in Rule30Realization. No literature-priority claim is made. The essay is \
prepared for human review and has not been sent to the prize API.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1208482393,ExpressionUUID->"25a568a6-87a2-527f-535a-810032572145"],

Cell["\<\
The terminal rewrites can change a finite row's right edge without changing \
its center trace. For example, the two rows with black cells only at \
positions {2,3} and {2}, respectively, have identical center traces at every \
time. Their left edges agree; this does not refute recovering a left edge \
from the trace. The general theorem allows every common initial left half and \
every shared finite prefix before the displayed terminal word.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->28909615,ExpressionUUID->"00d7f70d-8d0e-ae34-30ec-ef5724b8f51e"],

Cell["Exact response equivalence", "Section",
 CellID->193832008,ExpressionUUID->"2f3e8453-9762-68ea-56c5-1e1d22173a30"],

Cell["\<\
Index a right-half state x by 0,1,\[Ellipsis] for physical positions 1,2,\
\[Ellipsis] . Define\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->684317082,ExpressionUUID->"5902d484-617f-9570-0f31-cdee100bfc5a"],

Cell["\<\
    Q(x)(k) = x(k) XOR (x(k+1) OR x(k+2)).     F_b(x)  = (b XOR (x(0) OR \
x(1))) :: Q(x).\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->528485616,ExpressionUUID->"56e0e12c-de7d-6135-7e9f-f889db08301f"],

Cell["\<\
The second formula is exactly one Rule 30 step with left boundary bit b. Two \
right states have equal head outputs for every boundary-input sequence and \
every time if and only if, for every n, both the head and the OR of the first \
two cells of Q^n(x) agree. The infinite response tree is therefore replaced \
by two observations along one deterministic orbit. The theorem places no \
finite-support restriction on this equivalence.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1392207227,ExpressionUUID->"5708d032-2150-04f2-7d37-ca7c50e56ed8"],

Cell["\<\
For every common finite prefix, terminal 011 followed by zeros can be \
replaced by 01, and terminal 101 followed by zeros by 11. Both replacements \
preserve responses to all boundary inputs for all times. A separate induction \
shows that this equivalence remains sound with actual feedback through the \
center: if initial left halves, including the center, agree, they agree at \
every later time. Thus both terminal rewrites preserve the complete center \
trace in the full Rule 30 system, including when both starting rows are \
finite.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->705466992,ExpressionUUID->"3b7807e8-9504-b25a-6907-9eb21cb600a0"],

Cell["\<\
No converse from a single prescribed driver is proved. In fact, right states \
1 and 11 have the same head output forever under the constantly zero driver, \
but are not universally response equivalent. This driver is not asserted to \
extend to a finite whole-line orbit. A second example, packed states 42 and \
70, agrees through thirteen observations for every driver but is not \
universally equivalent. Checking only 2W-1 observations is insufficient at \
W=7.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1350516518,ExpressionUUID->"166f8969-5e31-e8e5-3e14-21335bc5bacf"],

Cell["A finite actual-feedback counterexample", "Section",
 CellID->907546795,ExpressionUUID->"234f1fb7-07c6-bba9-5174-96f1b27009f3"],

Cell["\<\
There is a stronger failure of the converse from one realized input. Let the \
common initial left half, including the center, be all white, and put black \
cells at {1} in one row and {1,2} in the other. Their initial right halves 1 \
and 11 are not universally response equivalent. With the white initial \
center, one step sends them to 11 and 101. The terminal rewrite above proves \
these new right halves universally equivalent. The entire left halves still \
agree, so feedback soundness applies at every subsequent time.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->814772321,ExpressionUUID->"6ee45b93-a0df-d1f6-169c-52dcd16ba3dc"],

Cell["\<\
Thus both the complete center traces and complete right-head traces agree \
forever, although the initial controlled response classes differ. Both starts \
are finite and nonzero. The kit's finite-configuration version of Jen's \
period-one result proves that neither common trace is eventually constant. \
This is an actual whole-line feedback example, without assuming an externally \
constant input. Their left edges are both position 1, so it does not refute \
left-edge recovery. No claim about their eventual periodicity is made.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1054628905,ExpressionUUID->"0a3edd72-e0d3-62fb-3bc5-d53f916630c1"],

Cell["Why the support and observation hypotheses matter", "Section",
 CellID->2043779557,ExpressionUUID->"6ec9a758-58be-624c-16f6-3f02594b3096"],

Cell["\<\
Write an infinite row from its head toward the right. The two starts\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->17830494,ExpressionUUID->"23253b8d-1c99-a6bf-52da-b2d7952d0c85"],

Cell["    x = 1100(1101)\[Ellipsis]     y = 0110(01)\[Ellipsis]", "Text",
 CellID->1589986527,ExpressionUUID->"6e84829b-9667-6594-16bb-1bc523124ffe"],

Cell["\<\
use parenthesized blocks repeated forever. Both complete Q orbits have period \
dividing eight. Their head traces differ, but the OR of the first two cells \
is the same nonconstant sequence at every time. At time two, the next OR, of \
cells one and two, differs. The common OR alone cannot recover the head or \
the next OR. These states are not universally response equivalent, because \
that relation observes the head as well.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->943101875,ExpressionUUID->"569e173f-6b36-b240-7ea5-8f790ec000aa"],

Cell["\<\
A third start, 1100(0001)\[Ellipsis], has a head trace of least period two, \
whereas its complete Q orbit has least period eight. All three starts are \
proved to have infinite support. A power-of-two orbit period cannot replace \
finite support in a proposed head-period detection theorem. No finite-tail \
conjecture is refuted or proved by these examples.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->67888809,ExpressionUUID->"15a3e88e-0b57-fa6e-4350-01e8aee3c8b4"],

Cell["\<\
The proofs use explicit temporal profile tables, verify their scalar update \
at every depth, and then identify all iterates by induction. The finite \
search that found the tables is not a proof premise. No part of this essay \
excludes an eventual center period or proves a Rule 30 Prize problem.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1432876021,ExpressionUUID->"779c57de-bb93-d239-1da4-4e983e27a103"],

Cell["Independent finite controls", "Section",
 CellID->876231958,ExpressionUUID->"2867ea9b-6e8d-517b-581c-03c50b382041"],

Cell["\<\
The first calculation compares terminal rewrites in 104 complete Rule 30 \
experiments with random common left halves and prefixes. The second compares \
all three profile tables with built-in Rule 30 on finite causal cones. The \
third checks eight actual-feedback examples, including the all-white left \
half, at both the center and the right head through time 128. These controls \
do not replace the unbounded kernel proofs.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1020575516,ExpressionUUID->"76dfbc62-92ac-7b6e-1ee4-364c275949b4"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

